From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toon Verwaest <verwaest@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:07:00 +0200
Subject: [preparser] Validate formal parameters even when body has syntax
 error

Previously, `PreParser::PreParseFunction` skipped propagating
`inner_scope->language_mode()` to `function_scope` and skipped calling
`ValidateFormalParameters` whenever the function body had a syntax
error.

As a result, lazy functions like
`function f(eval) { "use strict"; break; }`,
`function f(a, a) { "use strict"; break; }`, and
`let f = (eval, a = 1) => { "use strict"; };` reported the later body
error instead of the earlier formal parameter error, disagreeing with
eager parsing (`ParserBase::ParseFunctionBody`).

Always propagate `inner_scope->language_mode()` and run
`ValidateFormalParameters` in `PreParser::PreParseFunction` even when
the body has an error.

